/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A turbocharger has a turbine wheel and a compressor wheel on one shaft, held in an oil-fed bearing housing.
   - The energy comes from exhaust enthalpy, which the turbine extracts as the gas cools and expands; a turbocharger is an exhaust-driven supercharger.
   - Two manifold methods are common.
   - A single turbo must work from low to rated speed, so its size is a compromise.
   - Boost threshold is the lowest engine speed, at a given load, where the exhaust flow can spin the turbine fast enough for the compressor to exceed atmospheric pressure.
   - Turbo lag is a time.
   - Lag is felt as a pause followed by a surge, worst after a gear change or a spell of light-load cruising.
   - Matching a turbocharger means choosing a compressor and turbine that work well together over the engine range.
   - Lag-reduction hardware attacks inertia and exhaust volume.
   - The bearing housing gets pressurised oil from the engine; the film supports the shaft and carries heat away from the hot turbine end.
*/
