/* book Worked example 2: Q: A small turbo rotor (turbine wheel, shaft and compressor wheel) has a moment of inertia J = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ kg·m². At a throttle step it must spin up from 30 000 to 150 000 rev/min, and the turbine has, on average, 1.8 kW more power than the compressor and bearings absorb. Estimate the lag. How much shorter is it with a lighter rotor of 30 percent less inertia?
   1. Convert speeds: ω₁ = 30 000 × 2π/60 = 3142 rad/s; ω₂ = 150 000 × 2π/60 = 15 708 rad/s.
   2. Energy to store: ΔE = ½J(ω₂² − ω₁²) = 0.5 × 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ × (15 708² − 3142²) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ × (2.467 × 10⁸ − 0.099 × 10⁸) = 2369 J.
   3. Time at 1800 W of surplus power: t = ΔE ÷ P = 2369 ÷ 1800 = 1.32 s.
   4. With J reduced by 30 percent, ΔE and t fall by 30 percent: t = 0.7 × 1.32 = 0.92 s.
   Answer: About 1.3 s of lag; a 30 percent lighter rotor cuts it to about 0.9 s, which is why light wheels and ball bearings matter. */
