/* book Worked example: Q: A turbocharged engine takes air at 300 K and 1.0 bar. The compressor pressure ratio is 1.6 and its isentropic efficiency is 0.70; take γ = 1.4. An air-to-air intercooler of effectiveness 0.80 is cooled by ambient air at 303 K. Ignoring the small pressure drop across the cooler, find the compressor outlet temperature, the density ratio without the intercooler, the intercooler outlet temperature, the density ratio with it, and the extra air mass it gives.
   1. (γ − 1)/γ = (1.4 − 1)/1.4 = 0.286.
   2. T₂ = T₁ [1 + (rp0.286 − 1)/ηc], with T₁ = 300 K, rp = 1.6 and ηc = 0.70. Since 1.60.286 = 1.1437, T₂ = 300 [1 + 0.1437/0.70] = 361.6 K.
   3. Density ratio without intercooler = rp × T₁/T₂ = 1.6 × 300/361.6 = 1.327.
   4. Intercooler outlet: ε = (T₂ − T₃)/(T₂ − Tc,in), so T₃ = T₂ − ε(T₂ − Tc,in) = 361.6 − 0.80 × (361.6 − 303) = 361.6 − 46.9 = 314.7 K.
   5. Density ratio with intercooler = 1.6 × 300/314.7 = 1.525.
   6. Extra air mass = 1.525/1.327 = 1.149, about 15 percent more charge at the same boost. A real core loses a little pressure, so the true gain is slightly smaller.
   Answer: T₂ = 361.6 K; density ratio 1.33 without intercooling; T₃ = 314.7 K; density ratio 1.53 with it; about 15 percent more air mass. */
